---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { AXE_TAGS, COLOR_SCHEMES, LIGHTHOUSE_PACKAGE, VIEWPORTS } from '../../scripts/audit.mjs';
---

<Base
    title="Methodology"
    description="How the faheemnaeem.dev accessibility audit works, in plain language."
>
    <h1>How the audit works</h1>
    <p class="lede">
        Plain-language notes on what is tested, with which tools, and what the
        results do and do not prove.
    </p>

    <section aria-labelledby="what-title">
        <h2 id="what-title">What gets tested</h2>
        <p>
            Every site listed in my shared site list, from the main portfolio to
            each subdomain app. Once a week (Monday 06:00 UTC), and whenever I
            start it by hand, a GitHub Actions workflow visits each site's home
            page.
        </p>
        <p>
            If a site does not answer (its address does not exist yet, it times
            out, or it returns an error status), it is marked
            <strong>Not deployed yet</strong> and skipped. Most apps are still
            being built, so this is expected and is not counted as a failure.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="tools-title">
        <h2 id="tools-title">The tools</h2>
        <h3>axe-core</h3>
        <p>
            axe-core, by Deque Systems, is an open-source rule engine that
            inspects a rendered page and reports problems such as missing text
            alternatives, low colour contrast or buttons without a name. It runs
            inside a real Chromium browser driven by Playwright (a browser
            automation library), at these settings:
        </p>
        <ul>
            {
                VIEWPORTS.map((v) => (
                    <li>
                        {v.name}: {v.width} pixels wide
                    </li>
                ))
            }
            <li>colour schemes: {COLOR_SCHEMES.join(' and ')} mode</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            That makes four runs per site. A problem found in several runs is
            listed once, with the runs where it appeared. Rule sets used:
            {' '}<code>{AXE_TAGS.join(', ')}</code>.
        </p>
        <h3>Lighthouse</h3>
        <p>
            Lighthouse, by Google, loads the page in headless Chrome (a browser
            without a window) on an emulated phone and scores four categories
            from 0 to 100: performance, accessibility, best practices and SEO
            (search engine optimisation). The version is pinned to
            <code>{LIGHTHOUSE_PACKAGE}</code> so scores stay comparable. 90 or
            more is good, 50 to 89 needs work, below 50 is poor.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="wcag-title">
        <h2 id="wcag-title">WCAG levels, briefly</h2>
        <p>
            WCAG (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines) is the W3C standard for
            accessible websites. Version 2.2 is current. Each requirement, called
            a success criterion, has a number such as 1.4.3 and a level:
        </p>
        <dl class="levels">
            <dt>Level A</dt>
            <dd>The basics. Without these, some people cannot use the page at all.</dd>
            <dt>Level AA</dt>
            <dd>
                The usual legal and industry target, and mine. Adds things like
                4.5:1 text contrast, visible keyboard focus and touch targets of
                at least 24 by 24 pixels.
            </dd>
            <dt>Level AAA</dt>
            <dd>The strictest level. Not required for whole sites.</dd>
        </dl>
        <p>
            Some axe rules are <strong>best practices</strong>: good advice that
            is not a WCAG requirement. They count towards the total, but not
            towards the badge.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="impact-title">
        <h2 id="impact-title">Impact and the badge</h2>
        <p>
            axe rates each problem as minor, moderate, serious or critical,
            based on how badly it blocks people. A site shows the
            <strong>WCAG 2.2 AA</strong> badge only when the latest audit found
            zero serious and zero critical problems in all four runs.
        </p>
        <p>
            Automated tools catch only part of all accessibility problems. The
            badge means "no serious automated findings", not a full conformance
            claim. I also test by hand with a keyboard, a screen reader and 200%
            zoom.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="data-title">
        <h2 id="data-title">Where the data lives</h2>
        <p>
            Each run saves one JSON file in the repository
            (<code>apps/a11y/src/reports/&lt;date&gt;.json</code>) and commits it,
            so the full history is public and diffable. This site is rebuilt
            from those files and has no tracking and no client-side JavaScript.
            The newest report is also at <a href="/latest.json">latest.json</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
            When a problem disappears between two audits, the site page lists it
            under "Fixed issues" with a link to the code changes between the two
            audited commits.
        </p>
    </section>
</Base>
